.TH UICTL 1 "2026-08-20" "uictl 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
uictl \- find, inspect, and drive running macOS GUI applications
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uictl
.I subcommand
[options]
.br
.B uictl
.RB [ \-h | \-H | \-\-help | \-\-HELP | \-? ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uictl
locates running applications, lists and activates their windows,
enumerates connected displays, screenshots windows or displays
(optionally with a numbered accessibility-element overlay), walks their
accessibility tree, clicks, types, sends key combinations, waits for
elements to appear, runs OCR, samples pixel colors, and reads/writes the
clipboard.
.PP
A small background daemon (auto-started on first use, listening on a Unix
domain socket at \fI~/.uictl/uictl.sock\fR) holds Accessibility / Screen
Recording permission grants and the element-id cache across invocations.
It can also show two small on-screen windows of its own: a brief toast per
command, and a live activity-log window (\fBlog show\fR) with the option
to export that log as JSON.
.PP
Every subcommand prints exactly one JSON object to standard output and
exits 0 on success, non-zero on failure. This output is intended to be
consumed by scripts or AI agents as readily as by a human.
.B uictl
can also run as an MCP server over stdio; see the
.B mcp
subcommand below.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B daemon \fR[start|stop|status]
Manage the background daemon. \fBstart\fR is invoked automatically by any
other command if the daemon isn't already running.
.TP
.B permissions \fR[--request]
Check, or trigger the OS prompts for, Accessibility and Screen Recording
permission.
.TP
.B apps \fR[--all]
List running applications.
.TP
.B displays
List connected displays: id, global-coordinate bounds (can be negative
for a display left of/above the primary one), main-display flag, and
points-to-pixels scale. \fBindex\fR matches what \fBscreenshot --screen\fR
expects.
.TP
.B windows \fR[--app <selector>]
List on-screen windows, optionally filtered to one application. A name
substring matches, and aggregates windows from, every running instance
whose name contains it (e.g. both the old and new process right after an
app restarts). Each window includes a \fBdisplayId\fR matching one from
\fBdisplays\fR.
.TP
.B activate --app <selector> \fR[--window <id>]
Bring an application, and optionally a specific window, to the front.
.TP
.B screenshot \fR(--window <id> | --app <selector> | --screen <n>) [--out <path>] [--annotate] [--role <AXRole>]
Capture a window or display to a PNG file. With \fB--annotate\fR, overlays
a numbered box on every accessibility element and returns a JSON legend
mapping numbers to element ids, roles, titles, and frames.
.TP
.B elements \fR(--window <id> | --app <selector>) [--role <r>] [--title <substr>] [--max-depth <n>] [--max-elements <n>]
Walk a window's accessibility tree; returns each matching element's id,
role, title, value, and on-screen frame.
.TP
.B click \fR(--at <x,y> | --element <id>) [--window <id>] [--app <selector>] [--button left|right|center] [--double] [--count <n>] [--hover-cursor]
Click at a screen point or on the center of a specific element. If
\fB--window\fR or \fB--app\fR is given alongside \fB--at\fR, the point is
relative to that window's current top-left corner instead of an absolute
screen point (ignored/rejected together with \fB--element\fR, which uses
the element's own position). Restores the cursor to its pre-click position
afterward unless \fB--hover-cursor\fR is set. When clicking \fB--element\fR,
the JSON response includes a \fBverification\fR field
(\fBchanged\fR/\fBunchanged\fR/\fBunavailable\fR) reporting whether the
element's AX state could be diffed before and after — a successful exit
status does not by itself mean the click had an effect.
.TP
.B move --at <x,y> \fR[--window <id>] [--app <selector>]
Move the mouse cursor without clicking. \fB--window\fR/\fB--app\fR make
\fB--at\fR window-relative, as with \fBclick\fR.
.TP
.B scroll --at <x,y> \fR[--window <id>] [--app <selector>] [--dx <n>] [--dy <n>]
Scroll at a screen point. \fB--window\fR/\fB--app\fR make \fB--at\fR
window-relative, as with \fBclick\fR.
.TP
.B type \fR<text> [--element <id>]
Type text into the focused control, or into a specific element (tries
setting its value directly first, falls back to focus + synthesized
keystrokes).
.TP
.B key \fR<combo>
Send a keyboard shortcut, e.g. \fIcmd+shift+4\fR or \fIctrl+alt+delete\fR.
.TP
.B wait\-for \fR(--window <id> | --app <selector>) [--role <r>] [--title <substr>] [--timeout <seconds>]
Block until a matching element appears, or the timeout elapses.
.TP
.B ocr \fR(--image <path> | --window <id> | --app <selector>) [--region <x,y,w,h>]
Recognize text in an image file or a captured window, optionally
restricted to a region.
.TP
.B pixel --at <x,y> \fR[--window <id>] [--app <selector>]
Sample the color of a single screen pixel. \fB--window\fR/\fB--app\fR make
\fB--at\fR window-relative, as with \fBclick\fR.
.TP
.B clipboard \fR(get | set <text>)
Read or write the system clipboard.
.TP
.B log show
Open the on-screen activity log window: a live table of every CLI/MCP call
this daemon has handled, with a button to export it as JSON. The daemon
has no Dock icon or Cmd\-Tab entry (it runs as a background/accessory
app), so re-run this to bring the window back if it gets buried behind
others.
.TP
.B log export \fR[--out <path>]
Export the activity log as JSON (defaults under
\fI~/.uictl/exports/\fR). Params/response values are the same
summarized/truncated/redacted form shown in \fBlog show\fR's table, not
necessarily the exact raw payload for calls whose output was longer than
the truncation limit.
.TP
.B mcp
Run as an MCP server over stdio, exposing every capability above as an MCP
tool.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-H ", " \-\-help ", " \-\-HELP ", " \-?
Show help. All five spellings are accepted at the top level and are
normalized onto ArgumentParser's native \fB\-\-help\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
List an application's windows, then screenshot it with element numbers:
.PP
.RS
.EX
uictl activate --app Safari
uictl screenshot --app Safari --annotate --out /tmp/safari.png
.EE
.RE
.PP
Click the element numbered 7 in that legend's \fIid\fR field, then confirm:
.PP
.RS
.EX
uictl click --element 12345-7
uictl elements --app Safari --title "Expected Label"
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.uictl/uictl.sock
Daemon IPC socket.
.TP
.I ~/.uictl/uictl.pid
Daemon process id.
.TP
.I ~/.uictl/daemon.log
Daemon stdout/stderr.
.TP
.I ~/.uictl/screenshots/
Default output location for \fBscreenshot\fR when \fB--out\fR is omitted.
.TP
.I ~/.uictl/exports/
Default output location for \fBlog export\fR when \fB--out\fR is omitted.
.SH SECURITY
.B ocr\fR, \fBelements\fR, and \fBscreenshot\fR read whatever is genuinely
on screen, which can include passwords, tokens, or other sensitive text an
automated app happens to display; that content is not redacted (unlike
\fBtype\fR/\fBclipboard set\fR/\fBclipboard get\fR text, which is). It can
end up in the activity log (\fBlog show\fR, \fBlog export\fR) and in
screenshot files on disk. Manage and dispose of the activity log window,
its exports, and saved screenshots with the same care as any other
capture of your screen's contents.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success (the JSON response's \fIok\fR field is \fItrue\fR); non-zero
otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR screencapture (1),
.BR osascript (1)
